{
  // Typecheck the TESTS, which `tsconfig.json` does not.
  //
  // ## Why this file exists
  //
  // `tsconfig.json` is `include: ["src"]` and `rootDir: "src"`, both correct for
  // a build that emits `dist/`. The consequence nobody had noticed is that
  // `npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` — the command §9's "typecheck green"
  // ritual runs, and the one every phase has reported clean — says nothing
  // whatsoever about `tests/`.
  //
  // Found during the 6.2b close-out: a scripted edit inserted an import INSIDE a
  // multi-line import block in two test files, and `tsc --noEmit` reported clean
  // while those files were syntactically invalid TypeScript. The failing vitest
  // run is what caught it. A syntax error is the mildest version of that gap —
  // the suite always catches those, because esbuild has to parse the file to run
  // it. What the suite does NOT catch is a type error on a branch no test
  // exercises, since esbuild strips types without checking them.
  //
  // ## Why a separate file rather than widening `include`
  //
  // Adding `tests` to the main config would drag them into `rootDir` and emit
  // them into `dist/`, shipping test code in the production image. This config
  // is `noEmit` and exists only to be checked.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    // `rootDir` is inherited as `src`, which every file under `tests/` is
    // outside of. Widened here rather than removed so the base config keeps the
    // constraint that matters for the build.
    "rootDir": ".",
    // Vitest's `describe`/`it`/`expect` are imported explicitly in this suite
    // rather than relied on as globals, but the types still have to resolve.
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["src", "tests", "scripts", "vitest.config.ts", "vitest.workspace.ts"]
}
